{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"INVESTIGATION (Phase 1, analysis only — no code changed; operator-directed 2026-08-04 re-check of the \"one LLM call per pass\" claim). Verdict on the prior claim, the confirmed mechanism with citations, and a remedy recommendation. DO-NOT-IMPLEMENT scope respected.\n\n## (a) Verdict on the prior claim (\"each review pass only makes ONE LLM call, so within-run caching can never hit\")\n\nACCURATE for the measured shape, but INCOMPLETE as a general mechanism — and it is not, by itself, the root cause.\n\n- Accurate: in the two measured runs every pass is a single single-turn call (all five usage rows show requests=1; this ticket's own table). Code confirms the leaf-review shape: the finder chunks are `execution_mode=\"single_turn\"` unless the code-grounding tier (passes.py:319, pass1_chunk), the ISF/verifier/prereq-verifier/coach templates all declare `execution_mode: single_turn`, and a leaf ticket whose LLM-tier criteria fit ONE facet chunk yields exactly one plan-reviewer call (`registry.chunk_by_facet`, pass1.py:447). Five passes, five calls, five DISTINCT system prompts.\n- Incomplete: rebar's caching design was never premised on multi-call-per-pass alone. Within-run reuse is real and measured where >1 call shares a prefix: Pass-1 warm-then-fan-out (pass1.py:519-527, story 25fa) and the container warm-then-fan-out (pass1.py:237-267; story 0250 de-risk A measured 1 write / 8 reads across an epic fan-out), plus intra-call agentic reuse (this ticket: cache_read=386204 / 493547 on multi-step code reviews). So \"the cache is categorically wasted\" is wrong; it is wasted on the SINGLE-CHUNK LEAF review shape — which the measured runs happened to be, and which is the common case.\n\n## (b) Root cause of zero cache reads — three distinct mechanisms, with citations\n\n1. WITHIN one run, cross-pass reads are structurally impossible with the current marker placement. `cache_settings_for` (capabilities.py:611; settings at 649-668) sets only the instructions + tool-definitions breakpoints on single_turn calls, i.e. ONE breakpoint at the END of the whole system prompt. The three plan-bearing passes deliberately share a byte-identical LEADING segment — `shared_plan_prefix(plan)` (prompts.py:398-407: stance preamble + whole plan) is prepended for the finder (passes.py:290) and embedded as the verifier template's leading `{{shared_prefix}}` (plan_review_verifier.md:11) — but provider prompt caching keys entries on the EXACT bytes up to the breakpoint, and each pass's breakpoint sits after its pass-specific stance suffix. Three different suffixes → three different cache keys → 3 writes, 0 reads, every run. The byte-identity engineering of story 9374 is real but unexploitable: no breakpoint exists at the shared-prefix boundary. (The prereq-verifier is worse: its template puts its stance FIRST and `{{plan}}` at line 16, so even its leading bytes differ.)\n\n2. ACROSS runs on DIFFERENT tickets, reads are impossible BY DESIGN, not by accident. The marked prefix embeds the whole plan (that is the point — it is the stable bytes re-sent across a run's fan-out). Content-addressed caching cannot reuse a plan-bearing prefix across different plans. The measured experiment (1c39 then 9baf — two different tickets) therefore GUARANTEED zero reads regardless of any defect. This is the key correction to this ticket's framing: the prefix does not \"vary per ticket\" through instability — it varies because the plan is IN it, deliberately (stories 0250/8415/9374/25fa all build on that placement).\n\n3. ACROSS runs on the SAME ticket within the ~5-min TTL, the prefix IS byte-stable and WOULD read: plan_text is exactly title+description (det_floor.py:164-166), the templates are static files, and no timestamp/ordering-unstable content enters `_resolve_system` (passes.py:269-291 — variables are only {plan, shared_prefix}). The gate sequence just rarely re-reviews the same unchanged ticket within 5 minutes, so this path almost never fires in practice. (This ticket's dd27 sibling even recorded an ACCIDENTAL same-prefix cross-run hit inside the TTL during measurement — confirming mechanism 3 works.)\n\nCorollary: this ticket's AC \"the marked prefix for a given pass is byte-identical across TICKETS\" is unsatisfiable without evicting the plan from the marked prefix — which would destroy the within-run container/chunk reuse that warm-then-fan-out is built on, and bug f81d (closed wont-fix) already DISPROVED the move-the-plan-to-the-user-message alternative at mechanism level (both `*_cache_messages` and automatic caching mark the LAST message block, which ends with per-call-unique bytes). That AC should be re-scoped.\n\nAlso confirmed as stated in the description: the coach and ticket-digest passes write 0 because their marked prefixes (~150 / ~477 tok) sit below the model floor (bug f81d, evidence unchanged: plan_review_coach.md:20 puts `<!--volatile-->` two lines from the end; enrich's payload is per-ticket user text with no reusable prefix). And the diagnostic gap is real: `warn_if_cache_ineffective` fires only on read==0 AND write==0 (structured_run.py:452-455), so the write-every-call-never-read shape is invisible to telemetry — AC 4 of this ticket stands.\n\n## (c) Recommended remedy (NOT implemented)\n\nESCALATE-worthy part flagged below; the telemetry part is safe.\n\n1. SAFE / behavior-preserving: extend `warn_if_cache_ineffective` (or a small aggregate check over the run's usage records) to flag the \"wrote >0 on every call of a run, read 0 everywhere\" shape. Pure observability; keeps this class loud. (This ticket's AC 4.)\n\n2. THE REAL PRIZE — needs design + operator sign-off: place a SECOND cache breakpoint at the END of the shared plan-bearing prefix, so within one run the finder's write is READ by the Pass-2 verifier (and any other pass whose system starts with `shared_plan_prefix(plan)`), and a same-ticket re-review inside the TTL reads too. Mechanics to resolve: pydantic-ai's `anthropic_cache_instructions`/`bedrock_cache_instructions` mark only the LAST system block and `system_prompt` is a single string, so this needs either multi-block system assembly or an upstream knob — a runner-seam design change. It also wants the prereq-verifier template reordered to lead with `{{shared_prefix}}` (today its stance precedes `{{plan}}`, prerequisite_verifier.md:16), which REORDERS model-visible prompt bytes — not strictly behavior-preserving, so it needs a fidelity check and sign-off.\n\n3. Do NOT pursue cross-ticket prefix identity (the current AC 2 as written) and do NOT disable caching on single-call passes — the former conflicts with the within-run design and f81d's disproof; the latter is explicitly warned against in this description and would forfeit the container/agentic reads that demonstrably work.\n\nCost impact (rough, from this ticket's own figures): a leaf review currently pays ~9.2-9.5k cache-WRITE tokens (1.25x premium) for zero reads → ~2.3k token-equivalents of pure write-premium waste per review. With a shared-prefix breakpoint, a typical plan-bearing prefix (~2-3k tok) is written once and read (0.1x) by ~2 subsequent passes: saves roughly 0.9 × 2.5k × 2 ≈ 4.5k input-token-equivalents plus ~1.2k of avoided duplicate write premium — order 5-6k token-equivalents per plan review, and more on container reviews and TTL re-reviews. Exact numbers should come from the two-ticket prefix-render diff + live A/B this ticket's Testing section already prescribes."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785860671550489001,"uuid":"021f101f-1b09-4d64-8f75-36a0a6b219b3"}